div.p:
§ 2. ceterum: like the Gr. δ' οὖν, marking the return from a digression;
cf. Liv. xxxviii 55, ceterum ad hunc nimis aequum aut iniquum praetorem
reus extemplo factus L. Scipio.

p.note:
: this reading is established against the variant deprehendisset by Calig. 45, veluti profugos ac reprehensos in catenis reduxit;
cf. Phaedr. v 8, 3, elapsum semel non ipse possit Iuppiter reprendere:
Plaut. Most. 863, servi fugiunt, sed si reprensi sunt, etc.

: in Latin, as in Greek, adverbs are often used like adjectives;
see the examples given by Kühner-Stegmann, i 218 sq.: Nägelsbach, Lat.
Stilistik § 75.
